+// ======== 0012-r38-cells (kit r38, R77; campaign 3 memo-13-work/tom-ud-kernels.md §6, build notes tom-r38-build.md): the CPU
+// kernel costs that decide UD's A' half and Y1b, measured on the phone. No model, no GPU. Weights are random Q4_0 bytes of the
+// real shapes (finite fp16 scales in every layout), streamed cold from a 256 MB arena of matrix copies (call k uses copy
+// k % copies, at least 10 copies, so no matrix is in a cache when it is read again); x is random Q8_0 (the activation as
+// ggml quantizes it). Two thread sets: 4-7 (one pinned thread per cpu, 128-row units from a shared counter and a spin barrier
+// after each matrix, as l4) and cpu 4 alone (as R72 l1). Each set runs a chain stock | lever | stock | lever | ... | stock, so
+// every lever has a stock arm on each side (`ud trip`, stock_diff = |s1 - s2| / mean, as r37's cpu_diff). An arm is 0.3 s of
+// warm-up plus the wait for full clock (up to 1 s, ramp_ok; ggml arms up to 4 s, gtime's), then 1.0 s measured; thread 0 times every matrix (p10/p50/p90).
+// A canary line (r37's) follows every arm.
+//   udk1  `down` 4096 x 11008: stock (the probe's copy of ggml_gemv_q4_0_4x4_q8_0, the repack path) and K1 at TR 128 on real UD
+//         input masks (keep 0.5; four per subject from ud_dump, rotated per call; the kept lists are built per call, in the time)
+//   udr1  `gate` 11008 x 4096: stock, dense Y1b, and Y1b in place on each subject's real 70 % row list (|up|, cut to a multiple of 4)
+//   y1bd  4096 x 4096, 11008 x 4096, 4096 x 11008: stock, dense Y1b, and ggml's own mat-vec through a graph with the weights in
+//         the plain CPU buffer (--no-repack: ggml_vec_dot_q4_0_q8_0; the kit builds without llamafile), CPU_REPACK and CPU_KLEIDIAI
+//         (128 MB of copies each, ggml's threads through VOLLIE_POOL, as l4 / l5)
+// Lines are written to <dir>/x4-live-<blk>.txt as well (the app keeps them if a block hangs). Still off unless VOLLIE_PROBE is set;
+// nothing outside this file calls it, and no kernel of the CPU module changes.
